name = @vibe/builtin
version = 0.0.1
description =
  #|@vibe/builtin — the vibe language's own standard-library helpers
deps = {}

generated_hash =

// @vibe/builtin — the vibe language's own standard-library helpers
// (Int/Float/Double/Bool/String/Array/Option/Result/Char/Bytes/Cmp/
// StringView/Io/StringParser/Func/Iterator/LazyIter/AsyncIter). ADR-0070
// Phase 3, #897: the last remaining plain `index.vibe` directory in the
// LOW-RISK stdlib group (fs/json/http/shell/process/io/path/socket/symbol/
// time/cache/graph/module/optimizer/wasi were already migrated).
//
// Implicit-import investigation (blast-radius check before migrating):
// This package is NOT wired into the loader as a magic always-imported
// package -- which is why it is `@vibe/builtin` and not `@vibe/prelude`
// (#2105): "prelude" names the thing a compiler sneaks into every file, and
// this is not that. Every `.vibe` file that wants these names imports them
// explicitly (or
// relies on the compiler's OWN synthetic-injection builtins below) — a plain
// grep for this package across loader/*.vibe and checker/*.vibe turns up no
// "auto-import every file" hook. What looks like implicit access is
// actually TWO separate, narrower mechanisms that do not depend on this
// package's facade shape at all, so the .vpkg boundary does not interact
// with them:
//   1. `Result` no longer exists as a language- or library-provided type.
//      #1324 removed `result.vibe` (slice 4) and then the checker's
//      `inject_prelude_result` synthesis (slice 5) -- that compiler-side
//      symbol keeps its name, it is not this package -- so a program wanting
//      `Ok`/`Err` declares the enum itself like any other user enum.
//      Failure is carried by `Exception[E]` rows (ADR-0085).
//   2. `Option`/`Some`/`None` are genuine compiler builtins (`CtOption`),
//      never defined anywhere in this directory — `option.vibe` only
//      defines helper functions OVER `Option`, never the type itself.
// Neither mechanism reads this package's facade/contract text, so
// tightening the boundary here cannot change how `Result`/`Option` resolve
// in unrelated files. The one real leak the OLD plain-directory facade had
// (`fixtures/import_visibility_float_eq_test.vibe` importing the bare `eq`
// name that `float.vibe` exports but the old facade's own re-export block
// never listed — reachable only because a plain `index.vibe` directory's
// effective surface is leakier than its facade's own list, see
// loader/header_cache.vibe's validate-layout comment) is a real name that
// IS declared below (`let eq: (Float, Float) -> Bool`), so that fixture
// still resolves `eq` through the new contract-enforced surface.
//
// Trait declarations (Eq/Add/Div/Mul/Ord/Sub/Signed/Default, all from
// `builtin_traits.vibe`): a bodyless `opaque type Name` is satisfied through
// the same re-export boundary #847 gives any `export ./impl.vibe { name }`
// line. `collect_impl_type_defs` intentionally excludes `STrait`, but the
// loader resolves exported trait parameter kinds for constructor-kinded
// declarations such as `Functor[F[_]]`; nullary traits need only the name.
// This is NOT a first-of-its-kind choice:
// `lib/@vibe/core/index.vpkg` already established the pattern for `Hash`
// (`opaque type Hash`, satisfied by `maps.vibe`'s `export ./r#map.vibe {
// ..., trait Hash }` re-export) with the same reasoning spelled out in its
// own header comment. This package is simply the first to need this many
// of them at once. `builtin.vibe` (renamed from the old `index.vibe` facade —
// a directory may have exactly one of `index.vibe`/`index.vibei`/
// `index.vpkg`, #729/#897 — content otherwise unchanged) keeps the
// `export ./builtin_traits.vibe { Eq, Add, Div, Mul, Ord, Sub,
// Signed, Default, ... }` line that discharges these declarations.
// (`Hash` is deliberately NOT among them: #1844 removed this package's
// marker `Hash` — @vibe/core's method-bearing `trait Hash` in map.vibe
// is the one definition, and `[K: Hash]` users import it from there.)
//
// Builtin-intrinsic method declarations (`Int::to_double`, `String::
// utf8_length`, `String::utf16_length`, `String::unicode_length`): these
// four names have no `export fn` body anywhere in `int.vibe`/`string.vibe`
// (verified by grep) — they are pure compiler builtins recognized directly
// by the checker's builtin registry (`checker/builtin_sigs/builtins_misc.vibe`'s
// `lookup_int_to_double`; `builtins/declarations.vibe`'s `declare
// String::utf8_length(String) -> Int` etc.) with no vibe-source
// implementation to match against. The OLD `index.vibe` facade already
// re-exported them via `export ./int.vibe { ..., Int::to_double, ... }` /
// `export ./string.vibe { ..., String::utf8_length, ... }` — syntactically
// legal even though the named target has no definition in that file,
// because `check_contract` never verifies a re-export's target actually
// resolves (same #847 trust boundary as the traits above). `builtin.vibe`
// keeps those lines, so these four declarations are discharged the same
// way. (`Int::to_double` is additionally exercised directly inside this
// very package — see `double.vibe`'s float/int conversions — so its
// builtin status was never in doubt; it is real and load-bearing.)
//
// Every OTHER declaration below is a real `export fn` / `export let` (value
// alias) / `export struct` / `export enum` / `export type` living directly
// in one of this directory's sibling `.vibe` files, discovered by `.vpkg`'s
// unconditional implicit membership (#897/#855) — including names the OLD
// facade never re-exported at all (`iterator.vibe`, `lazy_iter.vibe`,
// `async_iter.vibe` were not mentioned by any block in the old facade, and
// several individual names — `String::split`/`to_upper`/`to_lower`,
// `cmp.vibe`'s `*_by_key` trio, `io.vibe`'s `ansi_escape`/`tui_*` — were
// exported but not re-exported either). Under the old plain-directory
// scheme those were reachable only via directory-shared scope from WITHIN
// the package; under `.vpkg` every exported name (from every production
// sibling) must appear here or it is a conformance error, so they are all
// promoted to real declared surface now.
//
// A BARE (unqualified) name that collides across sibling files — e.g.
// `abs`/`clamp`/`max`/`min`/`signum`/`square`/`lerp`/`is_nan`/`is_zero`/
// `is_negative`/`is_positive` across Int/Float/Double; `to_int`/`from_int`
// across Bool/Char; `to_string`/`from_string` across builtin_traits.vibe/
// char.vibe/string_view.vibe; `or`/`and`/`map_or`/`flatten`/`unwrap_or`/
// `unwrap_or_else`/`or_else`/`and_then` across Option/Result/string_parser;
// `get`/`length`/`filter`/`flatmap`/`zip`/`equals` across Array/Option/
// Bytes — CANNOT be declared once per colliding type: `contract_facade_
// source`'s allocation pass (contract/contract.vibe, the `allocated` array)
// tracks a declared NAME as satisfied by whichever impl unit is scanned
// first, with no signature disambiguation, so pushing two decl entries that
// share one name always leaves the second permanently unallocated and
// throws "some declarations have no implementation file" — a hard
// constraint discovered empirically while migrating this package (its
// scale of collisions, ~30 pairs, is unlike any prior migrated package).
// Each collision keeps EXACTLY ONE bare declaration below — the winner is
// whichever candidate the OLD `index.vibe` facade's own re-export block
// already exposed bare (verified against the original file, e.g. `abs`/
// `clamp`/`signum`/`is_zero` -> Int, `lerp`/`square` -> Float, `and_then`
// -> string_parser, `get`/`length` -> Array, `from_string` -> string_view);
// where NEITHER old-facade block exposed a bare winner (most of the pairs
// above), one candidate is picked so check_contract's NAME-only "exported
// but undeclared" sweep still covers every collision member (exactly
// `@vibe/core`'s documented policy for `length`/`is_empty`/`sum` — the
// LOSING side's function is still legal, still reachable from same-package
// sibling files, and — unless it truly has no `Type::` qualified form at
// all (char.vibe's to_int/from_int/to_string/from_string, and bytes.vibe's
// get/length once Array wins those two names) — still reachable externally
// via its own qualified name, all of which stay declared below regardless
// of the bare-name outcome. See each section's own comment for the
// specific winner/loser reasoning.
//
// `lazy_iter.vibe`'s `PullIterator[T]` and `async_iter.vibe`'s
// `AsyncIterator[T]` are implementation traits used only within this package.
// The public finite-iteration protocol is `Iterator[T]` below.

// --- types

// Compiler-provided declarations intentionally exposed by this package.
type Int
type Float32
type Float64
type Bool
type String
type Option[T]
fn String::join(parts: Array[String], separator: String) -> String
fn Int::to_string(_ value: Int) -> String

opaque type Eq
opaque type Add
opaque type Div
opaque type Mul
opaque type Ord
opaque type Sub
opaque type Signed
opaque type Default
opaque type Iterator[T]

type Byte
type Ordering
type ParseResult[T]
type Parser[T]
type ArrayView[T]
type StringView
type LazyIter[T]
type AsyncIter[T]

// --- builtin_traits (Eq/Add/Div/Mul/Ord/Sub/Signed impls live on the
// builtins directly; these are the generic combinators built on top)
fn cmp_eq[T: Eq](a: T, b: T) -> Bool
fn cmp_ne[T: Eq](a: T, b: T) -> Bool
fn num_abs[T: Signed + Ord + Sub](_ x: T) -> T
fn num_add[T: Add](a: T, b: T) -> T
fn num_div[T: Div](a: T, b: T) -> T
fn num_mul[T: Mul](a: T, b: T) -> T
fn num_sub[T: Sub](a: T, b: T) -> T
fn ord_max[T: Ord](a: T, b: T) -> T
fn ord_min[T: Ord](a: T, b: T) -> T
fn ord_clamp[T: Ord](x: T, min_val: T, max_val: T) -> T
fn num_clamp[T: Ord](x: T, min_val: T, max_val: T) -> T
fn to_string[T: Show](_ x: T) -> String
fn num_square[T: Mul](_ x: T) -> T
fn ord_between[T: Ord + Eq](x: T, min_val: T, max_val: T) -> Bool
fn default_require[T: Default](_ x: T) -> T

// --- int
fn pow(base: Int, exp: Int) -> Int
fn is_zero(_ x: Int) -> Bool
fn factorial(_ n: Int) -> Int
fn fibonacci(_ n: Int) -> Int
let max_value: Int
let min_value: Int
fn is_negative(_ x: Int) -> Bool
fn is_positive(_ x: Int) -> Bool
fn Int::abs(_ x: Int) -> Int
fn abs(_ x: Int) -> Int
fn gcd(a: Int, b: Int) -> Int
fn lcm(a: Int, b: Int) -> Int
fn Int::gcd(_ a: Int, b: Int) -> Int
fn Int::lcm(_ a: Int, b: Int) -> Int
fn Int::max(_ a: Int, b: Int) -> Int
fn max(a: Int, b: Int) -> Int
fn Int::min(_ a: Int, b: Int) -> Int
fn min(a: Int, b: Int) -> Int
fn Int::pow(_ base: Int, exp: Int) -> Int
fn Int::clamp(_ x: Int, min_val: Int, max_val: Int) -> Int
fn clamp(x: Int, min_val: Int, max_val: Int) -> Int
fn Int::is_odd(_ x: Int) -> Bool
fn is_odd(_ x: Int) -> Bool
fn Int::signum(_ x: Int) -> Int
fn signum(_ x: Int) -> Int
fn Int::is_even(_ x: Int) -> Bool
fn is_even(_ x: Int) -> Bool
fn Int::is_zero(_ x: Int) -> Bool
fn Int::factorial(_ n: Int) -> Int
fn Int::fibonacci(_ n: Int) -> Int
fn Int::is_negative(_ x: Int) -> Bool
fn Int::is_positive(_ x: Int) -> Bool
// builtin-intrinsic (checker/builtin_sigs/builtins_misc.vibe's lookup_int_to_double); no
// export fn body anywhere in int.vibe, discharged via builtin.vibe's
// re-export of int.vibe (see header comment)
fn Int::to_float64(_ x: Int) -> Float64
// #2344: popcount / ctz / clz are registry builtins (no export fn body in
// int.vibe either, same discharge route as Int::to_double above); select1 has
// a real body there because wasm has no select instruction to lower to. All
// four answer at the 63-bit width -- Int::popcount(-1) is 63, not 64 -- and
// define their zero cases: ctz(0) and clz(0) are 63, select1 returns -1.
fn Int::popcount(_ x: Int) -> Int
fn Int::ctz(_ x: Int) -> Int
fn Int::clz(_ x: Int) -> Int
fn Int::select1(_ x: Int, k: Int) -> Int
// #3127: opt-in checked arithmetic. The bare operators wrap (ADR-0105);
// these answer `None` exactly when the true result leaves the 63-bit range.
fn Int::checked_add(_ a: Int, b: Int) -> Option[Int]
fn Int::checked_sub(_ a: Int, b: Int) -> Option[Int]
fn Int::checked_mul(_ a: Int, b: Int) -> Option[Int]
fn Int::checked_div(_ a: Int, b: Int) -> Option[Int]
fn Int::checked_neg(_ a: Int) -> Option[Int]

// --- float (bare names are `export let` value aliases: (Float,...) -> ...).
// abs/max/min/clamp/signum/is_zero/is_negative/is_positive are ALSO exported
// bare by int.vibe (and abs/max/min/clamp/signum ALSO by double.vibe) —
// none of these bare Float forms were ever in the OLD facade's re-export
